TEXT 968 96 Left 2 !.param len=40 systems=2
TEXT -888 -688 Left 7 ;In this example there is a net with 230kV (60Hz) voltage level (slack,2 parallel lines, 2 loads, \nthree transformers, three generators with controllers for the synchronous machines. \nThe slack is at the right side \nIn the middle there are 2 loads with 322MW+100MVar and 590MW+100MVar \nand two capacitor banks with 200MVAR and 350MVAR\nAt time=0 only the slack is active\nAt time=0.5s the generator 1 is synchronized to the 20kV net and than it began\nto fed the net. At 10s it ends up with 700MW\nAt time=3.5s the generator 2 is synchronized to the 20kV net and than it began\nalso to fed the net at the end with 700MW again.\nAt time=15s generator 3 starts and deliver at the end also 700MW to the net.\nNOTE: The active power gradients are much to high for a physical machine.\nThis is only to held the simulation time small (for demonstration purpose)
TEXT -896 792 Left 7 ;Synchronization and Voltage angles:\nFor synchronization the generator voltage angle has to be nearly the same voltage angle as the net\nFor Generator 1 and 2 there are four phase angle meters for netangle and generator angle\nFor Generator 3 there is no need of a meter device because the angle at synchronization is nearly the\nsame as for the slack (The slack has angle 0 = reference). \nNOTE: The reference angle of the generators are at 180degrees (alpha_0) to coincide with the slack\nTo finetune the generators 1 and 2 voltage angles you have to do a simulation before , see the difference\nof voltage angles at synchronization time and than change the alpha_0 parameter of the generator.\nE.g. The net angle is -2 degrees and the generator 1 angle is -4 degrees you have to add 2 degrees to the\nalpha_0 parameter of the generator. (180+2=182degrees). Simulate again and do the same for generator 2\nNOTE: You can see that delivering active power from the generators to the net makes the voltage angle higher\nThe power transfer is always from the higher angle to the lower angle.
TEXT 952 8 Left 2 ;This device is a double system overhead line with 40km\n2x635mm2. To change the parameters of the single line\nto get a double system you have to set\nlength={len/systems} and cl={2*systems}\nlen/2 are for the inline R and L which are in parallel\nand cl is the multiplier for the capacitance \n(this is for the sum of 80km real linelength)
TEXT 848 464 Left 5 ;Load1
TEXT 1240 464 Left 5 ;Load2
TEXT 752 464 Left 5 ;Cbatt1
TEXT 1144 464 Left 5 ;Cbatt2
TEXT 1808 544 Left 2 ;NOTE: The on resistance\nof the switch has to be\nvery small since the 20kV currents\nof the generator are very high\nE.g. If Ron=0.01ohm the current of\n20kA gives power lost of 30*30*0.01=9MW !!
